  "summary_en": "European Commission report accompanying the 2020 AI White Paper. It assesses gaps in EU product safety and liability frameworks posed by AI, IoT and robotics, including connectivity, autonomy, opacity, data dependency, software updates and complex value chains. It proposes targeted, risk-based adaptations, such as strict liability with insurance for high-risk autonomous AI.",
  "key_provisions_or_claims": [
   "Product safety legislation should explicitly cover cyber risks, loss of connectivity and mental-health risks, e.g. from humanoid robots interacting with vulnerable elderly users",
   "New risk re-assessment should be required when self-learning products change substantially during their lifetime; human oversight requirements should be considered",
   "Product Liability Directive scope (software, 'putting into circulation') should be clarified and victims' burden of proof eased",
   "Strict liability coupled with mandatory insurance is considered for AI with specific risk profiles, such as autonomous vehicles, drones and delivery robots moving in public spaces",
   "A targeted, risk-based adaptation of the existing EU and national liability frameworks is proposed, rather than an entirely new regime"
  ],
